  "finalReply": "Key recovered (equivalent key decrypts all pairs).\n\n**Analysis:** The cipher is linear over GF(2): `state_{t+1} = M·state_t`, keystream bit = `row_i(M^t)·key`. Also `M^8 = I`, so the keystream byte repeats forever and the 8 equations per byte have rank 4 — 16 keys share the same keystream. Any solution of the linear system decrypts correctly.\n\n**Created `/app/attack.py`:**\n- `attack(known_pairs)` builds `A·key = b` over GF(2), where each keystream bit (`p ^ c`, MSB-first) gives row `row_i(M^{8j+i})` (fresh cipher per pair), solves via Gauss-Jordan with free variables set to 0, and falls back to 256-key brute-force verification.\n- Returns `numpy.ndarray` of 8 `uint8`.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `attack()` on `/app/known_pairs.txt` pairs returns e.g. `[1,1,1,0,0,0,0,0]` (keystream-equivalent to true `[1,0,1,1,0,1,0,1]`), and `StreamCipher(key).decrypt(ct) == pt` for all 5 pairs.\n- 20 random keys × varied lengths: recovered key decrypts all pairs; single-byte input already generalizes.",
